N('mohawk-river-mill-cohoes','Mohawk River Mill (Clifton Mill, the National Auto building)','Cohoes','conf','industry','standing',42.77352,-73.70129,'exact','view','walk','','1852',
  'Built in 1852 for Joshua Bailey\'s Mohawk River Mill: 350 feet long, four storeys and 600 workers, then the largest knitting mill in the world. A. E. Stimson\'s Clifton Company bought it in 1860. Fires in the 20th century cut it down to its present size, and the stone front was put on in the 1920s; it was long a National Auto hardware store.',
  'A cut-down mill behind a 1920s stone front at 122 Remsen Street, now shops and offices. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private commercial building: view from Remsen Street. Street parking downtown.',
  [SCHS],brick='?'),
N('first-methodist-cohoes','First Methodist Episcopal Church (Vineyard Community Church)','Cohoes','conf','worship','standing',42.77344,-73.70060,'exact','view','walk','','1860',
  'The Methodist society of Cohoes, organised in 1839, built its first church across the street in 1841 and a larger one here in 1848. In 1859 the trustees voted to take that down and build a bigger one, the church that stands, dedicated in 1860. It now houses the Vineyard Community Church.',
  'The 1860 church at 121 Remsen Street. Original fabric inside and service times not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church: see it from Remsen Street, or go in at service times (not checked).',
  [SCHS],brick='?'),
N('united-church-cohoes','Dutch Reformed Church (United Church of Cohoes)','Cohoes','conf','worship','standing',42.77335,-73.69992,'exact','view','walk','','1860',
  'The Dutch Reformed congregation of Cohoes was formed in 1837 and met in the seaming room of the Egberts and Bailey woollen mill until its first church was begun in 1838. That was torn down in 1859 for the present church, dedicated on April 11, 1860; its organ was given by the mill owners Egbert Egberts, David J. Johnston and John V. S. Lansing. In 1969 the congregation joined Silliman Memorial Presbyterian, whose 1896 church on Ontario Street is gone (Silliman Park is its site), to form the United Church of Cohoes.',
  'The 1860 church at 123 Mohawk Street. Whether the mill owners\' organ survives, and the interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church: see it from Mohawk Street. Service times not checked.',
  [SCHS],brick='?'),
N('st-bernards-cohoes','St. Bernard\'s Church','Cohoes','conf','worship','standing',42.77383,-73.69916,'exact','view','walk','','1863–1868',
  'St. Bernard\'s was the first Catholic parish in Cohoes; its first cornerstone was laid in 1847. The church that stands was begun in 1863 and dedicated in 1868. A winter storm in 1876 brought down the steeple; the tower seen now dates from a 1956–57 renovation, when it was lowered by twenty feet and given a granite Celtic cross.',
  'The church and rectory at 250 Ontario Street. The historical society says it is now privately owned: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private ownership: view from Ontario Street only. Street parking downtown.',
  [SCHS],brick='?'),
N('first-baptist-cohoes','First Baptist Church, 147 Mohawk Street','Cohoes','conf','worship','standing',42.77247,-73.69961,'exact','view','walk','','1850',
  'The First Baptist Church of Cohoes was founded in 1839 and built its first meeting house in 1840 at Remsen and Factory (now Cayuga) Streets, its congregation largely mill workers. It moved to this site at the south end of Mohawk Street in 1850, enlarged the church in 1872 and renovated it in 1911. Structural trouble led to a rebuilding in 1922–23, when the centre dome was taken off and the church took the look it has now.',
  'The church at 147 Mohawk Street as rebuilt in 1922–23. OpenStreetMap carries "First Baptist Church" only as an old name, so its present use was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'View from Mohawk Street. Owner and present use not checked. Street parking downtown; the Cohoes Public Library (the old St. John\'s) is a block south.',
  [SCHS],brick='?',apart='mohawk-street-houses-cohoes: houses farther along Mohawk Street; this is the church at number 147'),
N('cohoes-lock-15-towpath','Enlarged Erie Lock 15 and the towpath park, Vliet to Standish Streets','Cohoes','conf','canal','ruins',42.77833,-73.70344,'site','public','walk','canal','1836–1915',
  'One of the ten double-chamber limestone locks (9 to 18) that carried the enlarged Erie Canal, opened in 1842, up past Cohoes Falls; each lifted about ten feet. The canal through Cohoes was left behind when the Barge Canal opened through Waterford. Here a stretch of the old towpath is a linear park running from Lock 15 to Lock 14.',
  'Lock 15 southeast of Vliet and Summit Streets, and south of it a well-kept piece of canal bed with the sloping walls of the prism. The historical society names the towpath from Lock 15 to Lock 14 (at Standish Street) as one of the best canal walks in the city.',
  '',
  'City park path. Hours not checked. Street parking on Vliet Street; Lock 16 and the Harmony Mills are a block north.',
  [ERIE],brick='no'),
N('cohoes-locks-9-10-george-street-park','Enlarged Erie Locks 9 and 10, George Street Park','Cohoes','conf','canal','ruins',42.76467,-73.70242,'site','public','walk','canal','1836–1915',
  'The two lowest of the Cohoes locks of the enlarged Erie Canal, the first steps up from the junction with the Champlain Canal. George Street Park keeps the towpath between them. Sources differ on Lock 10: the Spindle City brochure says its western wall can be seen in the park, while the page used for the Atlas\'s Cohoes locks entry says it was dismantled in the 1950s.',
  'Lock 9 at the park\'s south end, north of Alexander Street, and the towpath north toward Lock 10. The pin is on Lock 10, where the bare-ground LiDAR shows a lock-shaped trench; how much stone is left was not checked.',
  '',
  'City of Cohoes park (George Street Park). Hours not checked. A mapped lot on St. Rita Lane is about 250 m north; street parking on George and Alexander Streets.',
  [ERIE,L('Lock-by-lock photos (eriecanal.org)','https://eriecanal.org/Lock9-18.html')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('ellsworth-monument-mechanicville','Col. Elmer E. Ellsworth Monument and Grave, Hudson View Cemetery','Mechanicville','sar','burial','standing',42.89806,-73.69222,'site','public','drive','','1861; monument 1874',
  'Elmer Ellsworth, a law clerk and close friend of Abraham Lincoln, was shot in Alexandria, Virginia, in May 1861 while taking down a Confederate flag, the first Union officer killed in the Civil War. He was buried in his hometown on May 27, 1861, in the family plot in Hudson View Cemetery. The monument over the grave went up in 1874. Its 300-pound bronze eagle was stolen in 1997 and came back the next year when an antique dealer recognised it.',
  'The 1874 monument with its bronze eagle over the Ellsworth and Denton family plot. The pin is the Register point for the monument.',
  'The first Union officer killed in the war, buried in a quiet mill-town cemetery.',
  'Hudson View Cemetery; who owns it was not checked. The Saratoga County History Center leads public walking tours there, and its page says parking is at the cemetery entrance on South Street. Cemetery hours and rules not checked.',
  [W('Col. Elmer E. Ellsworth Monument and Grave'),W('Elmer E. Ellsworth'),L('Saratoga County History Center: Hudson View Cemetery walking tour','https://brooksidemuseum.org/event/hudson-view-cemetery-walking-tour/')],brick='?'),
N('clifton-park-hotel','Clifton Park Hotel','Clifton Park','sar','hotel','standing',42.8658,-73.77036,'exact','view','drive','hotels','1820s–1970s',
  'A two-storey timber-framed hotel of the 1820s at the old crossroads of Route 146 and Route 9, given a two-storey wooden portico and a wing in the 1840s and renovated in the 1880s, when it had a dance hall. It stayed in business into the 1970s and has stood empty since; it was stabilised and re-roofed but left vacant. In 2025 a developer bought the 30-acre site for senior apartments and a gas station, and said the hotel would be restored as offices.',
  'The clapboard hotel with its Greek Revival detail and the two-storey piazza across the front, at 25 Old Route 146. Empty: interior not open.',
  'A stagecoach-era hotel waiting fifty years for its next life.',
  'Private, under redevelopment: view from Old Route 146 only. Do not go onto the site.',
  [W('Clifton Park Hotel'),L('Magic 100.5: Historic Clifton Park Hotel site redevelopment','https://www.albanymagic.com/news/capital-region/historic-clifton-park-hotel-site-redevelopment-big-changes-ahead/')],brick='no'),
